<h2 id="ifa-debut">Boox Picco Lands at IFA Berlin With Three Surprise Reveals</h2>
<p>In early September 2026, Onyx Boox used IFA Berlin — the European trade show where most upcoming consumer electronics make their first public appearance — to fill in the missing details around its pocket-sized e-reader, the BOOX Picco. The device was first <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/electronic-readers/onyx-boox-picco-will-cost-100-and-release-by-end-of-2026" rel="nofollow noopener">covered in late July</a> as a teaser, but IFA gave the company a stage to confirm three things readers had been guessing about for six weeks: a roughly <strong>$100 price tag</strong>, a switch from Android to <strong>Linux</strong> as the operating system, and the decision to ship <strong>without DRM</strong>. Together, those three details reshape how the Picco fits into the broader pocket-ereader category and what an EPUB reader can expect from it on day one.</p>
<p>The price lands within a dollar of the $99-$129 range I flagged as plausible in the <a href="/blog/onyx-boox-picco-pocket-epub-reader-2026/">original Picco primer</a>, but the OS and DRM decisions were genuinely new. Those two choices alone change the calculus for anyone weighing the Picco against the XTEINK X4 Pro, the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma 2</a>, or a refurbished older Boox reader.</p>

<h2 id="price">The $100 Price: A Direct Shot at the XTEINK X4 Pro</h2>
<p>Onyx Boox confirmed at IFA that the Picco will retail for around <strong>$100 when it launches in the next few months</strong>. Good e-Reader's IFA hands-on report, dated September 5, 2026, quotes Onyx representatives describing the price as tied to the device's deliberately limited hardware: a constrained processor, modest battery, and a small amount of RAM are all that you can buy for a Benjamin in 2026 if you also want an E Ink panel.</p>
<p>For EPUB readers, the relevant comparison is not against full-size Kindles or Kobos but against the pocket category the Picco was designed to enter:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>XTEINK X4 Pro</strong> — $99, magnetic attachment to your phone, 4-inch E Ink, but the older Linux-based firmware has limited EPUB feature support and no SD card slot.</li>
<li><strong>PocketBook Q mini</strong> — $235, phone-sized reader with similar 4-inch-class screen, runs Linux, supports a wider EPUB feature set including Adobe DRM out of the box.</li>
<li><strong>Boox Palma 2</strong> — $269.99, 6.13-inch Android-based reader, fully featured EPUB reader with DRM support but twice the price and larger form factor.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Picco's $100 price plus SD card slot plus EPUB support makes it the cheapest Boox-branded EPUB reader ever shipped, and one of the cheapest pocket EPUB readers of any vendor. For readers who curate their own DRM-free libraries from sources like <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">Standard Ebooks or Project Gutenberg</a>, the price is a strong reason to wait for the Picco rather than pay $169 more for the Palma 2.</p>

<h2 id="linux">Linux Instead of Android: Stability Trade-Off, App Ecosystem Closed</h2>
<p>The biggest architectural surprise is that Onyx Boox chose Linux over Android for the Picco. Onyx's existing readers — Palma, Page, Note Air, Tab Ultra — all run Android, which is what makes it possible to sideload KOReader, Crossink, or any other third-party EPUB app onto them. The Picco will not offer that.</p>
<p>The reasoning is cost-driven: a stripped-down Linux kernel lets Onyx hit $100 by requiring less RAM, a slower processor, and a smaller battery. It also lets Onyx keep the entire OS image internal — there is no published source, no community build pipeline, and the device will not accept sideloaded APKs. For locked-down readers this is fine; for tinkerers who rely on KOReader's CSS overrides and <a href="/blog/koreader-zen-ui-epub-2026/">advanced typesetting controls</a> to fix poorly formatted EPUBs, it is a real loss.</p>
<p>The Linux switch also closes off the workaround that lets some Boox users install Google's Play Store via a hidden menu. The Picco, on day one, will ship with whatever Onyx pre-installs and nothing else. That makes it a better fit for readers who want a single-purpose EPUB device and a worse fit for users who treat their ereader like a small Android tablet.</p>

<h2 id="no-drm">No DRM Means Library Books and Store Purchases Won't Work Out of the Box</h2>
<p>The third IFA reveal is the one with the largest practical consequence for how readers will use the device: <strong>the Picco will not support Adobe DRM or any other commercial DRM scheme</strong>. Early hands-on reviewers reported that the device has no Adobe Digital Editions integration, no Kobo account binding, no OverDrive/Libby sideload, and no Kindle app.</p>
<p>For DRM-free EPUB workflows, this is irrelevant. You can sideload an .epub file from <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">Project Gutenberg</a>, <a href="/blog/nasa-free-ebooks-epub-2026/">NASA's free e-books library</a>, Standard Ebooks, your own Calibre-managed library, or any direct-from-publisher download, and it will open and read normally. <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a> integration still works for library management, metadata editing, and format conversion — the DRM limitation is on the device side, not the management side.</p>
<p>For library books borrowed through Libby or purchased from Kobo, Google Play Books, or Kindle, the situation is more complicated. A standard sideload of an Adobe DRM EPUB will not authorize on the Picco because the device has no Adobe ID slot. The only path to reading those books on the Picco is <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">DRM removal</a> using Calibre plus the DeDRM plugin, which is a workflow choice rather than a default. If your reading life depends on library books and bookstore purchases, the <a href="/blog/libby-library-ebooks-ereader-2026/">PocketBook Q mini</a> at $235 or the Boox Palma 2 at $269.99 will be a better fit because both support Adobe DRM natively.</p>

<h2 id="form-factor">What Else IFA Hands-On Confirmed About the Hardware</h2>
<p>The IFA hands-on coverage added several hardware details that go beyond the July teaser:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3.97-inch grayscale E Ink display</strong> with a flat-bezel design and a built-in front light. The screen is described as responsive and minimalist, with no touchscreen reported in the early hands-on.</li>
<li><strong>Physical page-turn buttons</strong> on one side (up/down) plus a separate front-light button. There is no touchscreen, which limits on-device library navigation to button presses.</li>
<li><strong>SD card slot only — no internal storage</strong>. The device relies entirely on a removable microSD card for library storage. This is unusual in 2026 but keeps the price down.</li>
<li><strong>No audiobook player on the demo units</strong>. It is unclear whether Bluetooth and audio will be added before the final release. The current prototype supports neither Bluetooth nor wired audio.</li>
<li><strong>No web browser, no notifications, no email</strong>. This is a pure reading device by design.</li>
</ul>
<p>Compared to the original teaser, which left battery, RAM, and exact dimensions unspecified, IFA filled in most of the gaps. The Picco is shaping up to be the most pared-down reader Onyx has ever shipped — closer to a 2026 reissue of a 2010-era E Ink reader than to any current smartphone-replacement device.</p>

<h2 id="competition">How the Picco Changes the Pocket Ereader Category in 2026</h2>
<p>The pocket ereader segment grew fast in 2025 and 2026, driven by readers who wanted to leave their phone in their pocket and read EPUBs on a distraction-free screen. Three competitors currently define the category:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>XTEINK X4 Pro ($99)</strong> — the original pocket magnetic reader, Linux firmware, MagSafe phone attachment.</li>
<li><strong>PocketBook Q mini ($235)</strong> — phone-sized Linux reader with Adobe DRM and a more polished EPUB reader app.</li>
<li><strong>Boox Palma 2 ($269.99)</strong> — Android-based reader with full app ecosystem and DRM support.</li>
</ol>
<p>The Picco enters as a fourth option, and its <strong>$100 + Linux + no DRM</strong> combination puts it in a unique niche. It is cheaper than the PocketBook Q mini and the Boox Palma 2 by more than half, but it is also more limited than both because of the no-DRM and no-app decisions. For a reader whose library is DRM-free and who wants the cheapest possible Boox reader, the Picco is the obvious pick. For a reader who borrows library books or uses a commercial bookstore, it is a non-starter.</p>

<h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line: Wait for the Picco If You Read DRM-Free EPUBs</h2>
<p>The Boox Picco at IFA Berlin is the most affordable Onyx reader ever previewed, and the Linux plus no-DRM combination keeps the hardware bill down to a Benjamin. For DRM-free EPUB workflows — Calibre-managed libraries, Project Gutenberg downloads, <a href="/blog/nasa-free-ebooks-epub-2026/">NASA's free library</a>, direct publisher purchases, Standard Ebooks — the Picco will be a credible competitor to the XTEINK X4 Pro at the same price point, with the Onyx Boox brand behind it and an SD card slot the X4 Pro lacks.</p>
<p>If your reading depends on library books, bookstore-purchased EPUBs, or sideloading third-party reading apps like KOReader, the Picco is not the device for you. Spend the extra on a <a href="/blog/pocketbook-q-mini-phone-sized-ereader-2026/">PocketBook Q mini</a> ($235) for DRM support, or on a <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma 2</a> ($269.99) for full Android flexibility. But if you have a clean DRM-free library and want a $100 distraction-free pocket reader from a brand you can trust, the Picco is the most interesting new option in the segment.</p>
<p>Onyx has not yet announced a specific release date beyond "the next few months." A late 2026 or early 2027 release is plausible, mirroring the typical 2-4 month gap between IFA teaser and retail availability. The existing <a href="/blog/onyx-boox-picco-pocket-epub-reader-2026/">Picco primer</a> will be updated with verified pricing, shipping dates, and benchmarked battery life once the device ships.</p>
