<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Why does the Boox Picco run Linux instead of Android?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>Linux lets Onyx hit the $100 price point by requiring less RAM, a slower processor, and a smaller battery than an Android build would need. The trade-off is that the Picco will not support sideloaded Android apps such as KOReader, Crossink, or Google Play Store. The Linux OS image is also not published, so there is no community build pipeline for adding features later.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Can the Boox Picco read library books or Kindle books?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>No. The Picco will not support Adobe DRM, so it cannot authorize EPUB loans from Libby, OverDrive, or library catalogs that use Adobe Digital Editions. It also cannot read Kindle books, Kobo purchased EPUBs, or Google Play Books without using a DRM removal workflow such as Calibre plus the DeDRM plugin. The Picco is designed for DRM-free EPUB workflows only.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">What is the best alternative to the Boox Picco if I need DRM support?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>The PocketBook Q mini ($235) is the closest pocket-size reader with native Adobe DRM support, making it the right choice for readers who borrow library books. The Boox Palma 2 ($269.99) is the best Android-based alternative with full KOReader and Google Play Store support, at the cost of a larger 6-inch form factor and a higher price.</p>
</div>
